==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|elevatus|lang=la}}, past participle of {{term|elevare||to raise, lift up}}, from {{term|e||out}} + {{term|levare||to make light, to lift}}, from {{term|levis||light}}; see [[levity]] and [[lever]].

===Verb===
{{en-verb|elevat}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[raise]] (something) to a [[higher]] [[position]]; to [[lift]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[promote]] (someone) to a higher [[rank]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[ennoble]] or [[honour]]/[[honor]] (someone).
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To lift someone's [[spirit]]s; to [[cheer up]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[increase]] the [[intensity]] of something, especially that of [[sound]].
#: ''to '''elevate''' the voice''
# {{context|dated|colloquial|humorous|lang=en}} To intoxicate in a slight degree; to render tipsy.
#* Sir Walter Scott
#*: The '''elevated''' cavaliers sent for two tubs of merry stingo.
# {{context|obsolete|[[Latinism]]|lang=en}} To lessen; to detract from; to disparage.
#: {{rfquotek|Jeremy Taylor}}

